{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: current source has the bug at line 3017-3018 in RGBAStrippedMethod (no else branch), while the fix (15889) added `else q->opacity=OpaqueOpacity;` and also added it in tiled cases. So this source tree is at the pre-fix state for RGBAStrippedMethod (line 2940 case). Wait, but the tiled case at 3184 has else but no opacity set in else! Let me recheck: at line 3184, else loop sets red, green, blue, q++ — no opacity. That's the RGBATiledMethod. The fix added `q->opacity=OpaqueOpacity;` there too. Current code lacks it. So both are buggy.\n\nSo the vulnerability: When reading a TIFF where image->matte is False (no alpha), the RGBA strip/tile read path leaves q->opacity uninitialized. Then writing as PTIF resizes and VerticalFilter uses p[j].opacity for source->matte? Actually if source->matte is false, the else branch sets q[x].opacity=OpaqueOpacity and doesn't read p[j].opacity. So how does MSan flag it? Wait, the MSan report shows VerticalFilter reading uninitialized at line 1201, which is `pixel.opacity+=weight*p[j].opacity;` inside `if ((source->matte) || (source->colorspace == CMYKColorspace))`. So source->matte must be True during resize. But in the RGBAStrippedMethod path, q->opacity is set when image->matte is true. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. The bug is about \"opacity channel is not assured to be initialized\". Perhaps the scenario: The image has multiple frames. One frame sets matte=True, another frame doesn't. Hmm.\n\nActually let me re-read: in RGBAStrippedMethod, `if (image->matte) q->opacity=...`. So if image->matte is True, opacity is set from TIFFGetA. That seems initialized. Unless TIFFReadRGBAStrip returns data where the alpha byte is... no, that's from libtiff.\n\nWait, actually maybe the issue is different. Let's look more carefully at the current code. Hmm, line numbers: The error says ReadTIFFImage at tiff.c:2994 is SetCacheViewPixels. In current source line 2994 is SetImagePixels. The line numbers differ from the error because the error came from a slightly different revision. In the error, line 2994 = SetCacheViewPixels. So the buggy revision may be even older than the current source! Let me check. The error trace's tiff.c line numbers: 2994 SetCacheViewPixels, and VerticalFilter resize.c:1201, ResizeImage 1402, WritePTIFImage tiff.c:3843. Current source line 3843? Let me check. WritePTIFImage at 3780ish, ResizeImage call around 3825. Hmm 3843 could be WriteTIFFImage call. Let's check what current lines are.\n\nActually the description and error are from the original oss-fuzz report (2019). The provided `/src` may be the *fixed* version or the buggy version? We saw the fix at 15889 is in history (hg log shows it) and the current code at line 3017 has the buggy pattern without else. Wait, the hg log showed changeset 15889 as part of history — meaning the fix is applied in history. But then the current source should contain `else q->opacity=OpaqueOpacity;`. Let me check more carefully. The fix diff was against older line numbers (~3204). Current line 3017. Let me grep for \"else\" after line 3017.\n\nLine 3017: `if (image->matte)`; 3018: `q->opacity=...`; then 3019 `p++;`. No else. So the current source does NOT have the fix. But hg log lists changeset 15889... Hmm, maybe the repo has been modified/reverted deliberately for this task (they reverted the fix to recreate the vulnerability). Indeed the task is a synthetic vulnerability: they took graphicsmagick and reverted the fix. Let me confirm by checking `hg status`/diff against tip.\n\nActually the repo working dir may have uncommitted changes reverting the fix. Let me check `hg status` and `hg diff`."}}
